    "readingMinutes": 4,
    "wordCount": 798,
    "excerpt": "Welcome to The Boring Edit — a calm corner of the internet where I document what I'm learning, building, reading and thinking about. Here's what lives here and why it exists.",
    "contentHtml": "<p>Every personal website is a promise to the future. This one is mine: a quiet\ncorner of the internet where I document what I&#39;m learning, what I&#39;m building,\nwhat I&#39;m reading, and what I can&#39;t stop thinking about. No algorithm, no\nengagement metrics, no hot takes written to expire in a week — just notes,\nset in warm ink, that I&#39;ll be able to reread in five years without cringing\n(too much).</p>\n<p>My name is <strong>Nirmit</strong>, and this is The Boring Edit.</p>\n<figure class=\"post-figure\">\n  <img src=\"/images/desk.svg\" alt=\"A desk with a lamp, coffee and a sheet of paper\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n  <figcaption>fig. 01 — the desk, where every post starts as a plain markdown file</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"what-lives-here\">What lives here</h2>\n<p>The site is organised around a few recurring kinds of writing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Articles</strong> — proper write-ups on code, systems and things I&#39;ve gone deep\non. The technical meat of this site.</li>\n<li><strong>Journey</strong> — a running log of where I am: what I&#39;m working on, what I&#39;m\nstuck on, and what changed since the last entry. Honest, not curated.</li>\n<li><strong>Roadmaps</strong> — the plans themselves. Numbered series that unfold part by\npart, so you can follow along — or catch up later, in order.</li>\n<li><strong>Resources</strong> — the books, articles, tools and links worth keeping. If it&#39;s\non this site, it earned its place.</li>\n<li><strong>Philosophy</strong> — the why behind the what. Opinions about building, learning\nand living with software, held loosely enough to be corrected.</li>\n<li><strong>Interesting things</strong> — the loose ends: a demo, a quote, a chart, a bug\nthat taught me something. The stuff that doesn&#39;t fit a category but\ndeserves to exist somewhere.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>None of these require perfection. This is a notebook, not a newspaper.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-boring\">Why &quot;boring&quot;</h2>\n<p>The name is deliberate. <em>Boring</em> is what happens when a thing is understood,\nreliable, and calm — and those are exactly the qualities I want in the\nsoftware I build and the words I write. The most durable technology is\nboring. The best writing doesn&#39;t shout. A boring edit is the version of a\nparagraph you reach after cutting everything that was only there to impress.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The flashiest thing on this page is a single loud orange. Everything else\nis paper, ink, and hairlines.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So this site will stay deliberately small: fast static pages, a dot grid for\ntexture, no trackers, no pop-ups, no newsletter pop-over asking for your\nsoul. Just RSS if you want to follow along the old-fashioned way.</p>\n<figure class=\"post-figure\">\n  <img src=\"/images/journey.svg\" alt=\"A winding dotted route with numbered waypoints\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n  <figcaption>fig. 02 — the route: one post at a time, in order</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"how-this-will-work\">How this will work</h2>\n<p>The writing here is plain <strong>markdown files</strong> on my machine. A small build\nstep compiles them into this site — math typeset, code highlighted, every\npage prerendered to static HTML. When I write something new, the host simply\ngets new pages added to it. There&#39;s no CMS behind this, no database, no\nserver quietly billing me by the hour.</p>\n<p>A few commitments to make this worth visiting:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Series over scattered posts.</strong> Bigger topics — a language, a system, a\ndeep skill — will run as <em>tracks</em>: numbered parts you can binge from\npart 01, regardless of when each part was written.</li>\n<li><strong>Learn in public.</strong> Half-finished understanding is welcome here, clearly\nlabelled as such. Corrections are welcome even more.</li>\n<li><strong>Write for the reread.</strong> Every post should still make sense in a year —\nto you, and to future me.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"post-figure\">\n  <img src=\"/images/compass.svg\" alt=\"A paper compass rose pointing north\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n  <figcaption>fig. 03 — the compass: opinions held loosely, direction held firmly</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"the-philosophy-stated-once-up-front\">The philosophy, stated once up front</h2>\n<p>If the site has a constitution, it&#39;s these three lines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slow is smooth.</strong> Depth beats frequency. I&#39;d rather ship one post that\nstill matters in a year than thirty that don&#39;t survive the week.</li>\n<li><strong>Own the Means.</strong> The words live in markdown files I control, compiled to\npages I host, on a domain I own. If a platform dies, nothing here dies\nwith it.</li>\n<li><strong>Show the work.</strong> Roadmaps include the wrong turns. Resources include the\nones I abandoned and why. A journey log with only wins is a highlight reel,\nnot a journey.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"post-figure\">\n  <img src=\"/images/shelf.svg\" alt=\"A bookshelf of resources with one loud orange book\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n  <figcaption>fig. 04 — the shelf: resources that earned their place</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"where-to-start\">Where to start</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>If you want the ongoing story, the <strong>Journey</strong> entries will accumulate\nhere over time — this post is entry zero.</li>\n<li>Big topics will appear as <strong>Tracks</strong> on the tracks page, each part numbered\nso you can pick up exactly where you left off — the site even remembers\nwhat you&#39;ve read, locally, in your own browser.</li>\n<li>The <strong>shelf</strong> will grow as resources earn their place.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That&#39;s the tour. First real entries are on the way — see you at part one.</p>\n",
